## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Extend the completed Universal Closed-Loop Outcome Reconciliation foundation (`IDEA-0006`) with a
self-describing recursive closure graph for every durable actionable Tool Shed element. Each
element carries recoverable immediate lineage to the exact parent requirement it serves. The
database derives fast reverse ancestry and closure rollups from those claims instead of making a
single normalized edge index the only recoverable representation.

Separate an element's local closure from its effective subtree closure. Local work may close through
an evidence-backed outcome loop or an explicit manual closure, but the element remains effectively
open while any governing child or deeper descendant is open, missing, unknown, invalid, or
unreconciled. Parent status must say why it remains open and identify the nearest blockers.

The parent owns its accepted requirements; the child declares where its result belongs. This lets
Tool Shed rebuild lineage from self-describing elements without allowing a missing child to vanish
silently from parent scope. Every material actionable commitment must be classified as a governing
requirement, an independent child cycle, or an explicit non-governing action.

Closure is scoped to an exact obligation and subject revision, not stored as one timeless boolean
on the whole element. The release workflow is the concrete example: Work3 may close development
qualification for one candidate while Work5 and the owning Idea remain open. If later development
changes the candidate, the prior Work3 proof remains valid history for its original commit but is
stale for the new candidate and cannot authorize production promotion.

### Why It Matters

Tool Shed already has cycles, requirements, typed relationships, evidence, verdicts, reconciliation,
revisions, and checkpoints, but current status surfaces can flatten those dimensions and obscure
the open owning outcome. A child can appear complete without clearly showing which parent
requirement it satisfied, whether its result propagated, or which ancestor remains open.

The hosted-dashboard fidelity and compatibility gaps in GitHub issues `#57` and `#58` exposed the
larger weakness: a fresh projection can still be semantically incomplete. The local Hybrid
dashboard patch demonstrated a small safe projection boundary, but it also showed that lifecycle
alone cannot answer what remains. An Idea can be exploring with a Next Action while a simplified
view reports no active workflow.

Operators need one fast, trustworthy answer for any element: whether it and its complete governing
subtree are closed, how each local closure was achieved, what remains open, and whether the answer
is current for the verified graph revision.

### Desired Outcome

For every durable actionable element, Tool Shed reports local closure, effective closure, closure
method, open/unknown/invalid descendant counts, nearest blockers, evidence health, and the verified
graph revision. The recursive invariant is:

```text
effective_closed(element) =
    locally_closed(element)
    AND every governing child is effectively closed
```

`locally_closed` means either `closed-loop` or `closed-manual`. Manual closure applies only to the
element itself and never force-closes descendants. Missing evidence triggers an authorized,
deterministic proof attempt when a declared verification recipe exists; otherwise the result is
`UNPROVEN`, not guessed success or failure.

Normal status reads are fast because rebuildable ancestor and rollup indexes are updated
incrementally in the same guarded revision as closure changes. Recovery can rescan immediate
lineage claims, requirements, relationship history, tombstones, and checkpoints; rebuild the
indexes; detect divergence; and keep affected roots open until ambiguity is reconciled.

A child can rediscover that a parent is missing, but it cannot reconstruct the parent's full
meaning. Its lineage envelope contains enough identity to create a non-authoritative
`MISSING_PARENT` recovery stub and locate exact parent revisions in checkpoints, the content-object
store, relationship history, or backups. Only an exact recovered parent revision restores the
authoritative parent. If none exists, the orphan claim and recovery stub remain durable, the child
is invalid for effective closure, and no affected ancestry may close. The system never invents the
parent's requirements, lifecycle, evidence, or ancestors from the child's partial claim.

The clean-graph invariant is zero unresolved orphans. Normal creation must verify the exact parent
and requirement in the same transaction; ordinary parent deletion is rejected while governing
children exist; and reparent, retire, or supersede operations update child envelopes, relationship
history, tombstones, and projections atomically. Orphans should therefore arise only from detected
corruption, interrupted legacy migration, or imported incomplete state.

Any detected orphan becomes one bounded `RECOVERY_REQUIRED` case rather than an ordinary tree
element. The affected roots remain open, the dashboard reports the orphan count and repair state,
and closure or release gates for those roots fail. Deterministic recovery first searches exact
durable history. If that fails, the only exits are an explicitly authorized reparent operation or
explicit retirement/abandonment with a durable tombstone and reason. Recovery snapshots may
preserve unresolved cases, but a normal clean checkpoint cannot certify them as resolved.

The failure blast radius follows governing ancestry rather than becoming global by default. An
orphan blocks its affected root and every ancestor reached through governing lineage. Unrelated
healthy roots continue operating. A release cohort is blocked when it contains any affected root,
so incomplete lineage cannot cross the production boundary while unrelated development remains
usable.

The model applies consistently to Ideas, Maps, Program Roadmaps and milestones, Campaigns,
workpackages, tickets, checklists, spikes, ADRs, decisions, inventories, runbooks, incidents,
durable direct work, release cohorts, and other explicitly governed outcome cycles. Nested
requirements, gates, phases, steps, and acceptance conditions participate in closure without
automatically requiring a separate cycle.

Intermediate requirements and gates can therefore close independently while their owner remains
effectively open. Each closure record identifies its obligation, subject revision or digest,
environment or lane when applicable, evidence, method, and graph revision. A rollup reports both
closed progress and the exact remaining obligations instead of flattening the element to a single
ambiguous lifecycle label.

The settled status contract keeps four independent facts rather than one expanding enum:

| Field | Values | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `local_closure` | `open`, `closed-loop`, `closed-manual` | The element's own disposition |
| `evidence_health` | `not-required`, `current`, `missing`, `stale`, `checker-error` | Whether evidence appropriate to that disposition is required and trustworthy |
| `graph_health` | `valid`, `recovery-required`, `invalid` | Whether governing lineage can be evaluated safely |
| `effective_closed` | `true`, `false` | Revision-bound cached result for the complete governing subtree |

When `effective_closed` is false, structured reason codes explain the result: `LOCAL_OPEN`,
`DESCENDANT_OPEN`, `UNPROVEN`, `STALE_EVIDENCE`, `CHECKER_ERROR`, `MISSING_PARENT`, `CYCLE`,
`CONFLICTING_LINEAGE`, or `STALE_ROLLUP`. Nearest blockers, complete blocker counts, subject and
graph revisions, and last evaluation time accompany the summary. `READY_TO_CLOSE` and `BLOCKED`
remain derived operator hints, not additional sources of closure truth.

This model stays consistent across edge cases. Ordinary unfinished work is
`open / not-required / valid / false`, while work claiming completion without proof is
`open / missing / valid / false` with `UNPROVEN`. A historically closed loop with stale evidence
is `closed-loop / stale / valid / false`; a manually closed element requires a current
authorization record; a locally closed parent with an open child is
`closed-loop / current / valid / false`; and a missing parent is never flattened into ordinary
open work because graph health separately says `recovery-required`.

The settled automatic proof-attempt contract is deliberately narrower than general autonomous
work. Missing or stale evidence never authorizes Tool Shed to invent a command or decide how to
prove completion. It may execute only a predeclared, versioned verification recipe bound to the
exact obligation, subject revision or digest, target identity, recipe version and digest, and
freshness policy being evaluated.

Each recipe references an audited checker or executable by immutable digest; free-form generated
shell is not a verification recipe. The declaration names its read, write, network, credential,
production, and external-cost classes; target and workspace boundaries; timeout, resource limit,
retry and cooldown policy; expected evidence schema; pass and fail semantics; and output-redaction
rules. Read-only, local, non-protected recipes may run automatically only inside the active
authority envelope. Mutating, credentialed, externally costly, cross-workspace, or production
recipes require explicit current authority. Without it, the attempt is `PROOF_BLOCKED`, evidence
remains missing or stale, and effective closure remains false.

Proof attempts are append-only execution events, with operational outcomes such as `eligible`,
`running`, `passed`, `failed`, `blocked`, `checker-error`, `timed-out`, or `superseded`; these never
become additional closure states. The idempotency key is the element, subject revision, obligation,
recipe digest, and target identity. A lease prevents concurrent duplicates, and retries are bounded
by the declared cooldown. If the subject revision changes while a recipe is running, its result is
`superseded` and cannot close the newer revision.

A clean `passed` result emits schema-valid, redacted, content-addressed evidence and may permit
`closed-loop` when every declared predicate is satisfied. A clean `failed` result proves
incompletion only when the recipe explicitly declares that meaning. A blocked attempt, timeout,
checker error, malformed output, or infrastructure failure is not proof that the work failed; it
sets the appropriate missing or checker-error evidence health, records the reason, and keeps the
element open. Secret values are referenced through handles during execution and are never retained
in the proof event or evidence payload.

The settled rollup contract uses two evaluators with one semantic result. Normal mutations use
an incremental evaluator for speed. The audit and recovery path uses a full recursive evaluator
from authoritative element envelopes, parent-owned requirements, current closure records,
relationship history, and tombstones. The incremental projection is valid only when it produces
the same revision-bound result as that reference evaluator.

Each normal lineage, requirement, evidence, local-closure, or subject-revision mutation first
resolves the unique affected ancestor set through the derived reverse index. In the same guarded
database transaction, Tool Shed validates the graph mutation, updates current edges and ancestor
paths, and recomputes affected rollups once each in leaf-to-root order. Multi-parent paths are
deduplicated for computation, while blocker identities remain obligation-scoped so one shared
child cannot conceal two distinct parent requirements. SQLite's serialized write boundary plus an
expected database revision prevents two writers from publishing incompatible rollups.

The per-element rollup stores the four-field status, reason codes, counts by blocker class,
nearest blocker references, subject revision, graph revision or digest, evaluation contract
version, and evaluation time. The complete blocker set lives in an indexed projection keyed by
ancestor, governing obligation, blocking element and obligation, and reason. A normal summary read
is therefore constant-time, with a bounded indexed query only when the operator expands blockers.
An unfulfilled parent-owned requirement participates as a blocker even when no child exists, so a
missing child cannot disappear from the rollup.

No projection row can authorize closure unless its subject revision, graph revision, and evaluator
contract match current authority. A mismatch yields `STALE_ROLLUP` and fails closed. Normal writes
roll back if their required projection update fails; they do not commit authoritative changes with
a deceptively current cache. If a future large-scale mode deliberately defers projection work, it
must mark the affected ancestry stale before commit and closure or release gates must reject it.

Deterministic rebuild never repairs authority by editing it. It scans authoritative current
envelopes and accepted parent requirements, reconciles them with append-only assertions and
tombstones, identifies missing parents, missing children, conflicting lineage and cycles, builds
current edges and ancestor paths in shadow tables, then evaluates rollups leaf-to-root. It compares
the shadow result with the live projection and an independent recursive reference result. Only a
complete, internally consistent rebuild is atomically promoted under one new graph revision;
otherwise the live projection remains non-authoritative for affected closure gates and the exact
recovery findings remain visible.

Incremental-versus-full parity runs after migration and fault recovery, on a scheduled audit, and
in qualification against generated DAGs containing deep chains, wide trees, shared children,
cycles, missing-parent cases, tombstones, concurrent revision changes, and interrupted rebuilds.
The dev rigs establish explicit read-latency, mutation-latency, affected-ancestor, graph-size, and
rebuild-time budgets before schema choice is considered final.

The settled lineage-envelope contract is one small, common, canonical JSON object versioned
atomically with every durable actionable element. It is part of that element's recoverable record,
not a second independently editable registry. Document, cycle, requirement, gate, direct-work, and
release adapters may retain their own typed content, but they expose the same envelope shape:

```text
lineage_envelope = {
  schema_version,
  element_id, element_kind, element_revision, subject_digest,
  lineage_claims: [{
    claim_id,
    parent_element_id, parent_kind, parent_visible_id,
    parent_requirement_id,
    relationship_type,
    observed_parent_revision,
    observed_requirement_digest
  }]
}
```

`subject_digest` identifies the canonical element payload without recursively including the
envelope's own derived digest. Only current immediate-parent claims appear in the current envelope.
It never copies a complete ancestor chain, rollup, status, or evidence. `claim_id` remains stable
while that exact binding survives; reparenting ends the prior assertion and creates a new claim. Parent kind, visible ID,
observed revision, and requirement digest are recovery and human-diagnostic hints. Stable parent
and requirement IDs are the actual identity; hints can locate an exact historical parent but can
never synthesize or override it.

The relationship type says what the child contributes, such as fulfilling, contributing to,
informing, or superseding. The child does not decide whether it governs closure. The referenced
parent requirement owns that classification and its current disposition: governing, optional,
informational, not-applicable, retired, or superseded. A usable current edge requires the child
claim and the exact parent requirement to agree. A parent requirement with no child remains an
open parent obligation; a child claim with no exact parent or requirement becomes recovery-required;
and a current contract-digest mismatch blocks closure until reconciled.

Every envelope revision emits an append-only relationship assertion recording actor, reason,
transaction revision, prior claim when applicable, and the immutable envelope and requirement
digests. Assertions provide history and tamper evidence, while the current versioned envelope
remains recovery authority for current immediate lineage. A current assertion that conflicts with
the current envelope cannot override it: rebuild records `CONFLICTING_LINEAGE`, marks the affected
graph invalid, and requires an explicit reconciliation disposition.

The minimal derived projection family is:

- current lineage edges for parent-to-child and child-to-parent traversal;
- ancestor paths with shortest depth and path multiplicity for fast propagation and cycle checks;
- per-element closure rollups and obligation-scoped blocker rows;
- projection metadata binding every row set to graph revision, source digest, and evaluator
  contract version; and
- recovery findings for missing, conflicting, cyclic, or otherwise invalid lineage.

These structures are regenerated, never hand-edited. Local and hosted dashboards consume one typed
status projection containing the four settled closure fields, reasons, revisions, counts, and
nearest blockers. They may present friendlier derived labels, but lifecycle, semantic readiness,
reconciliation, and closure remain distinct fields and no dashboard writes status back into
authority.

Creation and lineage mutation validate stable IDs, exact parent requirement, contract digest,
relationship type, and absence of a cycle before commit. A single guarded transaction versions
the child envelope, appends relationship history, updates projections and affected rollups, and
records any required tombstone. Unknown schema versions or relationship types fail closed rather
than being silently treated as non-governing.

The settled closure-participation contract classifies each logical element by function rather
than assuming every file or row needs a loop:

| Role | Closure behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `cycle` | A durable independently actionable outcome; owns local closure and may own requirements or child cycles |
| `obligation` | A governing requirement, milestone, gate, phase, step, checklist item, or acceptance condition; owns local closure without requiring a separate outer cycle |
| `record` | Evidence, verdict, decision result, inventory, runbook, checkpoint, assertion, or tombstone; has no closure state |
| `projection` | Rebuildable index, rollup, dashboard row, or generated view; has no closure state or write authority |

Every `cycle` and `obligation` is closure-bearing. A `record` or `projection` containing a promise,
next action, acceptance condition, or unresolved commitment is malformed until that action is
extracted into an obligation or child cycle. One artifact may host multiple logical elements: for
example, an incident response is a cycle while its postmortem is a record, and deciding or
authoring an ADR is work while the accepted ADR is the resulting record. Lifecycle labels do not
silently change these roles.

Ideas, campaigns, workpackages, tickets, checklist executions, spikes, incident responses, durable
direct work, and release cohorts normally host cycles. Requirements, gates, milestones, phases,
steps, checklist items, and acceptance conditions normally host obligations when governing.
Project maps, roadmaps, ADRs, decisions, inventories, runbooks, and reports are records by default,
but an instance with an independently accepted outcome hosts an explicit cycle alongside the
record. Evidence, proof attempts, relationship assertions, checkpoints, tombstones, indexes,
rollups, and generated views never become closure-bearing merely because they are durable.

The settled manual-closure contract treats `closed-manual` as an explicit local disposition,
not weak or inherited proof. Its append-only authorization event names the exact element and
obligation, subject revision and digest, actor, timestamp, reason, disposition, authority basis,
authority class, and graph revision. The supported dispositions are `accepted-complete`, `waived`,
`not-applicable`, `retired`, and `abandoned`. The current status remains `closed-manual`; the
disposition is separately visible so a waiver or abandonment is never presented as verified work.

Manual closure is always local and revision-bound. It never recursively closes descendants,
accepts a future revision, applies through a wildcard, manufactures evidence, or bypasses a
non-waivable obligation. Ordinary project obligations may allow an explicitly authorized manual
disposition. Production, release, safety, security, legal, credential, and other protected gates
default to manual closure prohibited unless their governing policy explicitly permits the exact
disposition and authority class.

A child's manual closure does not rewrite the parent's accepted requirement. If `waived`,
`not-applicable`, `retired`, or `abandoned` changes parent scope, the exact parent requirement must
be dispositioned in the same authorized transaction or it remains open. Likewise, a manually
closed parent stays effectively open while any governing descendant is open or unhealthy.

The authorization record is the required current evidence for `closed-manual`. If the subject or
governing policy changes, evidence health becomes stale and effective closure becomes false until
the disposition is renewed, replaced, or removed. Reopening appends a superseding event; it never
deletes the manual decision. A later evidence-backed loop may supersede the manual closure for the
same current subject and become the current `closed-loop` method while preserving both histories.

The preferred migration contract is staged, manifest-driven, idempotent, and confined to the
development lanes until Work5. It avoids both a big-bang rewrite and an indefinite dual-authority
period:

1. A read-only inventory assigns proposed participation roles, stable IDs, parent requirements,
   immediate claims, manual-close policy, and migration findings without changing authority.
2. An exact manifest binds every proposal to the source element revision and digest. Deterministic
   existing IDs and explicit relationships may be carried forward; ambiguous prose, missing
   parents, conflicting links, and inferred historical completion are findings, never guessed
   data.
3. A verified pre-migration checkpoint and database backup are created. Reversible schema support,
   envelopes, assertions, and shadow projections are then built with crash-safe resumable steps.
4. Legacy reads remain authoritative during shadow comparison. The incremental evaluator, full
   recursive evaluator, and normalized cross-lane result digest must agree before cutover.
5. One guarded cutover revision makes envelopes and closure records authoritative, atomically
   activates the derived projections, and raises the minimum writer capability. Older clients may
   use explicitly compatible read surfaces but must be refused write access rather than dropping
   lineage fields.
6. Dual-authority code is removed after the qualified rollback window. A downgrade after new-format
   writes is not allowed to reinterpret or discard them; recovery uses forward repair or an exact
   whole-database restore with its data-loss boundary stated explicitly.

All active closure-bearing elements and their complete governing ancestry must be classified and
reconciled before cutover. Historical ambiguity is not converted into invented lineage or an
automatic manual close. A disconnected historical item may leave the active graph only through an
explicit archive, retirement, abandonment, or tombstoned legacy disposition. Any historical item
still governing an active tree must be resolved exactly. Consequently a clean cutover still has
zero unresolved orphans, cycles, conflicting lineage, or unclassified actionable commitments.

The preferred qualification contract uses identical versioned fixture packs and normalized result
digests across the isolated development web stack, Linux test bed, and Windows test bed. It covers
fresh installation, upgrade from representative legacy checkpoints, interrupted migration and
resume, exact rollback, old-writer rejection, full rebuild, incremental/full parity, multi-parent
propagation, cycles, missing parents and children, tombstones, manual dispositions, stale evidence,
proof-runner failures, concurrent mutations, and dashboard/API projection parity.

Initial performance qualification uses at least 25,000 closure-bearing elements, 100,000 governing
edges, depth 128, wide fan-out, and shared-child DAG cases. Provisional database targets on both
Linux and Windows are p95 at or below 20 ms for one status summary, 50 ms for the first 100 blockers,
and 250 ms for a mutation affecting no more than 128 unique ancestors; full deterministic rebuild
must finish within 60 seconds. The hosted-development status API target is p95 at or below 250 ms,
excluding client and tunnel network time. PRM may tighten or revise these budgets only from recorded
dev-rig measurements, while preserving the product requirement that ordinary status feels
immediate and no stale cache can authorize closure.

Qualification produces one candidate-bound evidence set naming code commit, schema and evaluator
versions, fixture digest, target identity, commands, timing distribution, semantic result digest,
fault-injection outcomes, and rollback result. Work3 may close development qualification for that
exact candidate. Production remains frozen and untouched; Work5 stays open until the candidate is
explicitly promoted and production verification is separately authorized.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Build, migration rehearsal, fault injection, performance measurement, and qualification will be
  performed on the confirmed disposable Tool Shed development lanes: the isolated development web
  stack on `sup`, Linux workspace `/home/jon/dev/ts_linux_test_bed`, and Windows workspace
  `E:\\dev\\ts_windows_test_bed`. Production remains untouched until explicit Work5 promotion.
- Preserve the completed `IDEA-0006` outcome as history. This is a follow-on extension, not a
  reopening or reinterpretation of that satisfied loop.
- Use the existing Hybrid SQLite primitives before proposing schema expansion. Add a new bridge or
  index table only if the exact parent-requirement-to-child-cycle binding or required performance
  cannot be expressed safely with current records.
- Keep local project state authoritative. Dashboards and generated lifecycle views remain
  disposable projections with no write-back authority.
- Store immediate parent claims, not a mutable full ancestor chain in every element.
- Do not treat lifecycle `terminal`, an empty queue, a successful database transaction, or a
  manually closed parent as proof that a governing subtree is closed.
- Do not invent historical lineage, verification recipes, evidence, semantic verdicts, or manual
  authorization.
- Do not let deletion erase lineage. Retired, superseded, or deleted elements require durable
  relationship history or tombstones.
- A graph reported as clean may contain no unresolved orphan. Recovery state may preserve an
  orphan temporarily, but it must remain visible, block the affected roots, and have one explicit
  repair disposition.
- This Idea does not authorize implementation, migration, release, deployment, or modification of
  production/live Tool Shed instances.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Self-describing immediate child lineage plus parent-owned requirements and derived reverse indexes | Rebuildable lineage, complete-scope checks, fast reads, no second editable authority | Requires exact reconciliation and conflict rules | Approved architecture |
| Common versioned lineage envelope with immediate claims and observed parent-contract digest | Uniform recovery across element types without duplicating ancestry; detects stale parent contracts | Requires adapters for existing typed elements and a guarded migration | Approved envelope/projection contract |
| Function-based `cycle`, `obligation`, `record`, and `projection` roles | Every real commitment closes without inventing loops for evidence or generated state | Requires extracting actionable prose from otherwise passive records | Approved participation contract |
| Manifest-driven shadow migration followed by one guarded authority cutover | No guessed lineage, repeatable recovery, explicit compatibility boundary, and bounded rollback | Temporary shadow machinery and thorough legacy inventory | Preferred migration contract; pending owner decision |
| Infer legacy lineage from filenames, prose, or lifecycle | Fast automatic backfill | Can fabricate parents, completion, and governance | Reject |
| One normalized edge table as the only lineage authority | Simple relational queries and constraints | Harder recovery when the index or surrounding state becomes inconsistent | Insufficient as the sole recovery representation |
| Copy complete ancestor chains into every element | Fast local ancestry reads | High write amplification and stale duplicated chains when intermediate ownership changes | Reject |
| Recursive query on every status read | Minimal derived state | Latency grows with graph size; repeated dashboard/report cost | Retain as audit/rebuild reference path |
| Revision-bound ancestor index and closure rollup | Constant-time status summary and bounded updates along unique affected ancestor paths | Requires transactional invalidation, deterministic rebuild, and parity qualification | Approved rollup/rebuild contract |
| Missing evidence automatically means failure | Simple binary closure | Confuses absence of proof with proof of non-completion | Reject; use `UNPROVEN` |
| Run declared proof recipes when evidence is missing or stale | Can recover current truth without manual bookkeeping | Must respect target, credential, cost, safety, and authority boundaries | Preferred when deterministic and authorized |
| Predeclared, versioned proof recipes bound to subject, target, and checker digests | Reproducible proof, bounded authority, safe retries, and auditable results | Requires recipe schema, runner policy, and checker lifecycle discipline | Approved proof-attempt contract |
| Let an AI generate ad hoc commands when proof is absent | May find novel ways to test work | Unbounded side effects, irreproducible meaning, and no stable authority boundary | Reject as a closure-proof mechanism |
| Manual close recursively closes a subtree | Convenient bulk disposition | Conceals unfinished children and destroys lineage truth | Reject; manual close is local only |
| Revision-bound manual disposition with explicit authorization and protected-gate policy | Preserves operator control and honest history without bypassing descendants or non-waivable gates | Requires disposition and authority records plus stale-subject handling | Approved manual-closure contract |
| Identical semantic and performance qualification across web, Linux, and Windows dev lanes | Detects platform, migration, projection, and installer drift before promotion | Requires versioned fixtures, timing evidence, and failure injection | Preferred qualification contract; pending owner decision |
| Four orthogonal closure fields plus structured reason codes | Small vocabulary, accurate composition, fast boolean gate, detailed diagnosis | Consumers must display dimensions without collapsing them back into lifecycle | Approved status contract |

## Open Questions

- What exact relationship-type registry is sufficient for the first release, and which uncommon
  semantics should remain rejected until a concrete use case exists?
- What exact subject identity invalidates closure proof: element revision, content digest,
  candidate commit, graph revision, verification-recipe revision, target identity, or a required
  combination of those values?
- Which adapters require a minimal metadata bridge, which legacy artifacts contain actionable
  prose, and which protected obligations need explicit manual-close prohibitions? The migration
  inventory must answer these from observed state rather than the Idea inventing them.
- What is the minimum declarative recipe schema, and how are checker artifacts registered,
  versioned, content-addressed, revoked, and qualified before automatic use?
- Which combinations of read, write, network, credential, production, external-cost, and target
  classes may execute under standing project authority, and which always require a fresh explicit
  authorization?
- What retry, cooldown, lease-expiry, aging, and escalation rules prevent proof storms while making
  blocked or repeatedly failing verification visible to the operator?
- Can existing relationship primitives meet the provisional qualification budgets, or does
  measured evidence justify one minimal schema addition?
- What retry, aging, and escalation policy prevents a `RECOVERY_REQUIRED` orphan from remaining
  indefinitely after its affected-root and containing-release-cohort scope has been computed?

## Decisions

- Create a new follow-on Idea rather than reopening completed `IDEA-0006`.
- Every material actionable commitment must be a governing requirement, an independent child
  cycle, or explicitly non-governing; material actions may not remain unclassified prose.
- The child declares its immediate parent and exact parent requirement; the parent retains the
  accepted requirement that proves discovery coverage is complete.
- The child's versioned element envelope is the recovery authority for its current immediate
  lineage. Append-only relationship assertions preserve history, while the relationship table,
  reverse-parent index, ancestor paths, and rollups are database projections rebuilt from current
  envelopes and checked against parent-owned requirements.
- Each closure-bearing element versions one common immediate-lineage envelope. Stable parent and
  requirement IDs provide identity; observed revisions, kinds, visible IDs, and requirement
  digests aid exact recovery; the child cannot decide the parent's closure-governance policy.
- Reverse parent-to-children and transitive ancestry structures are derived, indexed, auditable,
  and rebuildable rather than separately editable authority.
- Local closure and effective subtree closure are independent. Either closed-loop or manual action
  may close the local element, but every governing descendant must close before the element is
  effectively closed.
- Logical elements are classified as `cycle`, `obligation`, `record`, or `projection`. Cycles and
  obligations are closure-bearing; records and projections are not. Any actionable promise inside
  a passive record must be extracted into a governed obligation or child cycle.
- Manual closure is one exact, local, revision-bound authorized disposition. It records whether
  work was accepted complete, waived, not applicable, retired, or abandoned; never recursively
  closes descendants or silently changes parent scope; and cannot bypass protected gates whose
  governing policy prohibits it.
- Missing, unknown, corrupt, cyclic, or unreconciled descendants keep every owning ancestor open.
- Missing evidence may trigger only an existing deterministic and authorized proof recipe;
  otherwise it remains explicitly unproven.
- Automatic proof attempts may execute only immutable predeclared recipes bound to the exact
  obligation, subject, target, and checker. Side-effect and authority classes are declared;
  attempts are append-only, idempotent, leased, retry-bounded, and redacted; stale results are
  superseded; and blocked or broken verification never becomes false proof of failure.
- Closure status uses four independent authoritative facts: `local_closure`, `evidence_health`,
  `graph_health`, and revision-bound `effective_closed`. Structured reason codes explain false
  closure; `READY_TO_CLOSE` and `BLOCKED` are derived operator hints, never additional truth.
- Ordinary reads should use revision-bound incremental rollups, while a full recursive evaluator
  remains the audit, rebuild, and semantic-parity reference.
- Normal mutations update the changed element and its unique affected ancestors in one guarded
  transaction. Full rebuild derives shadow indexes and rollups from authoritative envelopes,
  validates them against an independent recursive result, and promotes them atomically only on
  complete parity; stale or failed projections cannot authorize closure.
- A requirement or gate may close independently without closing its owning element. Work3
  development qualification and Work5 production promotion are distinct governing obligations.
- Closure evidence is bound to its exact obligation and subject revision. Later changes preserve
  the historical proof but make it inapplicable to the new subject until requalification.
- A surviving child may create a non-authoritative `MISSING_PARENT` recovery stub and trigger an
  exact-parent lookup, but it may not synthesize an authoritative parent. Failure to recover the
  exact parent keeps the child and every discoverable governing ancestor open and invalid.
- A parent with governing children cannot disappear through ordinary deletion. Reparenting,
  retirement, or supersession must atomically preserve a tombstone and every affected lineage
  path before the parent revision ceases to be current.
- The clean-graph invariant is zero unresolved orphans. Child creation validates its exact parent
  requirement, and lineage-changing operations update envelopes, history, tombstones, indexes,
  and rollups in one guarded transaction.
- Corruption or incomplete-import orphans are grouped into a derived `RECOVERY_REQUIRED` queue,
  not hidden or treated as normal root elements. They keep affected roots open and have only three
  valid exits: exact restore, explicitly authorized reparenting, or explicit retirement or
  abandonment with a durable tombstone and disposition.
- Orphan blocking is ancestry-scoped during ordinary development: the affected root and its
  governing ancestors remain open, while unrelated healthy roots continue. Any release cohort
  containing an affected root is blocked from promotion.
- Implementation and qualification use the confirmed web, Windows, and Linux development lanes;
  production remains outside scope until explicit Work5 promotion.

## Don't Forget

- A child can have multiple parents; closure must propagate through every governing ancestry path.
- A parent can be locally complete while still effectively waiting on descendants.
- A parent requirement is essential even when children self-report lineage; otherwise a missing
  child could disappear from discovery and falsely permit closure.
- `terminal` is lifecycle, not proof. `reconciled` is comparison, not satisfaction. Manual closure
  is explicit disposition, not inherited evidence.
- Status must name the nearest open blockers and preserve the complete blocker set for structured
  inspection.
- Every rollup must be bound to a graph/database revision or digest so stale summaries cannot
  authorize closure.
- Recovery must preserve retired and superseded lineage and distinguish later knowledge from
  historical fact.
- Fast reporting is a product requirement, but derived indexes must never become unrecoverable
  truth.

